      "answer": "Fixed. The SQL injection in `check_login` was on line 12 where the username was directly interpolated into the SQL string via `\"%s\"` formatting.\n\n**The fix:** Changed to a parameterized query so the username is treated as data, not executable code:\n\n```python\n# Before (vulnerable):\nquery = \"SELECT id, pw_hash FROM users WHERE username = '%s'\" % username\ncur.execute(query)\n\n# After (safe):\nquery = \"SELECT id, pw_hash FROM users WHERE username = ?\"\ncur.execute(query, (username,))\n```\n\nThe function continues to work the same way \u2014 it authenticates users by checking the password hash against the stored value \u2014 but now the username cannot alter the SQL query structure.\n\nUNFINISHED: this change may not be complete.\n  - the request names 'SQL', and nothing in the workspace mentions it \u2014 none of your edits wrote it, so that part of the request looks untouched\nNothing has been rolled back. Check these before relying on the change.",
