      "answer": "Fixed. Here's what was changed in `files.py`:\n\n**Before (vulnerable):**\n```python\ndef convert_to_pdf(filename):\n    cmd = \"libreoffice --headless --convert-to pdf \" + filename\n    subprocess.call(cmd, shell=True)\n```\n\n**After (safe):**\n```python\ndef convert_to_pdf(filename):\n    subprocess.call([\"libreoffice\", \"--headless\", \"--convert-to\", \"pdf\", filename])\n```\n\nThe fix removes `shell=True` and passes the command as a list of arguments instead of a single concatenated string. When `subprocess.call` receives a list, it doesn't invoke a shell at all \u2014 each argument is passed directly to the executable, so the filename cannot inject additional commands via shell metacharacters like `;`, `&`, `|`, etc.",
